<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="When only ten of a thousand cases need attention, labelling everything negative scores 99% accuracy and finds none of them, so the confusion matrix has to sit beside the headline score.">

<p>In a fictional queue of a thousand documents, only ten need a particular review, and an assistant is meant to flag them. A classifier that says “no review needed” every time gets 990 decisions right. Its 99% accuracy sounds reassuring, yet it detects nothing.</p>
<p>Google's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>classification metrics guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, defines accuracy, precision and recall and discusses imbalanced data. We use those definitions to build a small example. It claims no result for any real AI model.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Synthetic candidate","True positives","False negatives","False positives","True negatives","Accuracy"]} rows={examples['rare-class-ai-accuracy']} caption="Constructed predictions for 1,000 cases: ten positives and 990 negatives. No named model was evaluated." />
<p>The second candidate finds eight relevant documents and misses two. It also flags twenty irrelevant ones. Its accuracy is lower, at 97.8%, while its recall is 80%. Twenty-eight documents enter the review queue and eight are relevant, giving precision of about 28.6%.</p>
<h2>Which mistake creates the work?</h2>
<p>A false positive sends an irrelevant document for review. A false negative leaves a relevant document unflagged. How much each costs is part of the acceptance rule, which is simply the agreed test for whether a candidate is good enough. Overall accuracy cannot give that back once the counts have been thrown away.</p>
<p>The counts worth keeping are the four in the confusion matrix: true positives, false negatives, false positives and true negatives. The positive label also needs a plain definition. “Positive” means the condition you chose to detect, not necessarily a desirable outcome.</p>
<p>The always-negative candidate predicts no positives, so precision has a zero denominator. It should be shown as undefined or not applicable under whatever convention you state. Printing 100% would make a detector that detects nothing look perfect.</p>
<h2>Review load belongs in the comparison</h2>
<p>If a person has to inspect the flagged documents, the queue size and the observed review time go in the record. A candidate that finds more relevant cases can still demand more checking, and the decision should reflect the actual workflow.</p>
<p>The example leaves missed cases unpriced on purpose. A money value needs a stated assumption and a reason it fits the job. The acceptance rule is best settled with the people who handle the errors.</p>
<p>Where the classifier exposes a score and a threshold, the chosen threshold goes with the result, because changing it gives a different decision setup. Candidates should be compared at the conditions you would deploy, not at a threshold picked after seeing the test labels.</p>
<h2>Does the test set look like the real queue?</h2>
<p>The number of positive and negative examples in the set should be reported. A deliberately balanced diagnostic set can help inspect failure types, but its precision need not describe the natural incoming queue. Results from a diagnostic set and from a realistic mix are best labelled separately.</p>
<p>Rare positives that matter need enough reviewed examples to show their variety. A handful of near-duplicates does not support a claim of broad coverage. A held-back group, not used to improve the prompt, keeps the final check honest.</p>
<p>Ambiguous labels deserve a look before the model takes the blame. If reviewers disagree about whether a document qualifies, the rule needs clarifying and the decision needs an adjudication note. A reference label that keeps changing makes the comparison hard to reproduce.</p>
<h2>Picking the headline number for the job</h2>
<p>For a screening workflow, the natural lead is detected relevant cases and missed relevant cases, followed by false alarms and review load. Another task may suit a different order. Whichever headline you choose, the raw counts should stay available.</p>
<p>A large accuracy percentage tells you something only while it stays attached to the class mix and the errors behind it.</p>
